Code taken from Qt
------------------

The ritual files, the C++ in 'src/cutienator/occult/rituals', are
written by the dev tooling of cutienator, and 2,111 of them also hold
code taken from Qt's own source: code Qt's libraries carry under no
name, so that a ritual can call it. That code is Copyright (C) The Qt
Company Ltd., and it stays under the license of the file of Qt it
comes from. Each such ritual file names that license in the notice
above the code taken, as the file of Qt says it in its own SPDX line.
The texts of the licenses are in the folder LICENSES.

Most of it is licensed as most of Qt is, and all of it in QtCore,
QtGui and QtWidgets:

  LGPL-3.0-only OR GPL-2.0-only OR GPL-3.0-only

The ritual files of these parts hold code Qt licenses GPL-3.0-only:

  QtCanvasPainter, QtCharts, QtChartsQml, QtCoap, QtDataVisualization,
  QtGraphs, QtGraphsWidgets, QtGrpc, QtHttpServer, QtLottie, QtMqtt,
  QtNetworkAuth, QtQuick3D, QtQuick3DHelpers, QtQuick3DParticles,
  QtQuick3DPhysics, QtQuick3DRuntimeRender, QtQuick3DUtils,
  QtQuick3DXr, QtWaylandCompositor

and those of these GPL-3.0-only WITH Qt-GPL-exception-1.0:

  QtDesigner, QtQmlCompiler

A library summoned from the ritual files of such a part holds that
code, and so is under GPL-3.0-only, with the exception where Qt gives
it, when it is given to anyone. So is that part of Qt itself: Qt gives
these parts no LGPL.

The ritual files of QtCore5Compat for its codecs of Chinese, Japanese,
Korean and Tamil hold code that Qt's REUSE.toml puts under
BSD-2-Clause as well, for those who first wrote it, and each names
them in its notice:

  q_big5_codec, q_big5hkscs_codec, q_c_p949_codec, q_euc_jp_codec,
  q_euc_kr_codec, q_gb18030_codec, q_gb2312_codec, q_gbk_codec,
  q_jis_codec, q_sjis_codec, q_tscii_codec

The SPDX lines of Qt name 'LicenseRef-Qt-Commercial' as well, the
license Qt sells. cutienator has no such license, and gives the code
taken from Qt under the other licenses of each line only.
